<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edigu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1352">1352</a>–<a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Edigey, Yedigey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edigu was an emir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Mangit tribe</a> who, though not a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from about 1396 to 1411 through puppet khans. He defeated Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the former khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a> in 1399 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1408. His descendants founded the Nogai Horde, and he became a hero of Turkic epic poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edigu.jpg" alt="Edigu" width="320" height="589" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.12,_p.177_-_Edigu%27s_invasion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
